:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers key point 1 well. It frames breakeven AUM as fee revenue versus fixed costs such as audit, legal, accounting, index licensing, listing and board fees. It partly covers key point 2: fee waivers, expense caps and lineup economics show that the sponsor absorbs costs. However, it never says plainly that the sponsor's willingness and ability to keep funding losses is central, nor that size alone does not predict closure with certainty. Key point 3 has a substantive gap. It notes seed source (sponsor vs. third party), duration and concentration, but omits the key distinction that market-maker seed is temporary inventory that can be redeemed. It also does not say that investor seed is more lasting yet subsidized seed investors can leave. Its claim that long-unredeemed seed signals weak demand is a debatable framing, since seed being pulled is usually the risk. On governance, it mentions board minimum-AUM and liquidation policies but not that a series-trust board can replace the adviser as well as liquidate. The added indicators on flows, distribution and trading are useful but do not fill these central gaps.\"}","ttft_ms":4217,"type":"result","duration_ms":7285,"uuid":"28651537-e3ef-470b-b124-b402c1bd1ca3","ttft_stream_ms":548,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":548,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
